Short:        Doom 3, the 2004 first-person shooter
Uploader:     rtyp3r@yahoo.com (Aleksandar Vojnic)
Author:       Aleksandar Vojnic (R-Typer), Ladislav Vojnic (ladislawv)
Type:         game/shoot
Version:      1.0-beta6
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.0.0
Requires:     PiStorm, Raspberry Pi 4 (or 3), Emu68, RTG, Doom 3 1.3.1 data

===========================================================================
                    DOOM 3 FOR AMIGAOS 3   version 1.0-beta6
          id Software's Doom 3 running natively on a PiStorm Amiga
===========================================================================

Doom 3 (id Software, 2004) ported to AmigaOS 3. The game's code is 68k
code and runs on Emu68, the PiStorm's 68k emulation on the Raspberry Pi;
the graphics are drawn by the Raspberry Pi's VideoCore GPU through
ogles2.library (OpenGL ES 2.0 for AmigaOS 3), which is included. Based
on dhewm3/d3es, the GPL release of Doom 3's source code.

This is a beta. Please report what you find (see CONTACT), with the
file T:doom3-out.txt: the game's messages from its last run.

THE GAME'S DATA IS NOT INCLUDED. You need your own copy of Doom 3: the
nine pak00?.pk4 files of version 1.3.1 (GOG.com, Steam, or a boxed copy
with the 1.3.1 patch). The free 2004 demo's data (demo00.pk4) works too.
Doom 3: BFG Edition does NOT work: its data is in another format.


SUPPORTED HARDWARE - PLEASE READ
--------------------------------

This is for an Amiga with a PiStorm running Emu68. It is not for classic
Amigas, accelerator cards or 68k graphics cards on their own: Doom 3
needs the Raspberry Pi's processor speed and its GPU.

  * Tested: an A1200 with a PiStorm and a Raspberry Pi 4, Emu68
    1.1.0-beta.1, AmigaOS 3.2.3, Picasso96.
  * Raspberry Pi 3B/3B+: ogles2.library drives its GPU too, but Doom 3
    has not been tried on one yet. Expect much less speed (see
    PERFORMANCE).
  * Elsewhere, for example WinUAE, the game uses the ogles2.library
    installed in LIBS: (the included one drives only the Raspberry Pi's
    GPU). A user reports it running in WinUAE with QuarkTex NG's
    ogles2.library. Not tested by us.


FEATURES
--------

  * The complete Doom 3 single-player game, or the free demo, from your
    own data files.
  * Drawn by the Raspberry Pi's GPU at any 32-bit RTG screen size: 1280x720
    by default, 1920x1080 and more.
  * Four quality settings, Low to Ultra, from the game's own menu. The
    settings changed in the menus and the console are saved.
  * Sound, speech and music through Paula (the Amiga's own sound chip,
    the default) or AHI.
  * The game's speed follows the frame rate: the game runs as many tics
    a second as the Amiga can draw frames, between 20 and 60.
  * Only the screen sizes your RTG system has are offered in the menu.
  * The in-game computer screens take mouse clicks, as on the PC.
  * Starts from its icon or a Shell, with options as Tool Types or Shell
    arguments. Colour icons (GlowIcons).
  * Nothing is installed into the system: everything stays in the drawer.


REQUIREMENTS
------------

  * An Amiga with a PiStorm and a Raspberry Pi 4 or CM4 (a Pi 3B/3B+
    untested), running Emu68 with its VideoCore RTG driver.
  * AmigaOS 3.x (tested on 3.2.3) with Picasso96 or CyberGraphX.
  * A 32-bit RTG screen mode (16.7 million colours) at the size you want
    to play at. The default is 1280x720: check that Picasso96Mode has
    one, or start the game at another size (W= and H=).
  * Plenty of Fast RAM: tested with the 1.8 GB Emu68 gives a Pi 4.
  * About 1.6 GB free for the full game's data (about 500 MB for the
    demo's), and 40 MB for the drawer (30 MB without its source/).
  * ogles2.library 1.4 beta 3 and its shader compiler are included in the
    drawer's libs/, and used from there on the PiStorm whatever version
    LIBS: holds. LIBS: is not changed.
  * AHI 6 only for AHI sound (AUDIO=ahi).


PERFORMANCE
-----------

Doom 3 was made for PCs far faster than any Amiga: expect a playable, not
a smooth game. Measured on an A1200 with a Raspberry Pi 4 (its ARM at
2.2 GHz), Low quality, 12 lights:

  1280x720, Doom 3's own benchmark (timedemo demo1)    about 27 fps
  1280x720, the first level at its start, with sound   about 27 fps
  1920x1080, Doom 3's own benchmark                    about 19 fps

The benchmark replays recorded frames without the game's logic: real
play in busy scenes is slower. The 68k code (Emu68) is the limit more
than the GPU, so smaller screens help less than you might expect.
Shadows are off at every quality (they cost the most); "r_shadows 1" in
the console turns them on.

A Raspberry Pi 3 is much slower (its Emu68 and its GPU), its GPU unpacks
the compressed textures into 4 to 8 times the memory, and it has 1 GB in
all: start with QUALITY=low W=640 H=480 there. The drawer holds the
shaders compiled for the Pi 4; a Pi 3 compiles its own the first time
each is needed (a short wait), then keeps them.


INSTALLATION
------------

 1. Unpack the archive where you want the game, for example to Work:
       LhA x Doom3-Amiga.lha Work:
    This makes the drawer Doom3. It can be on any partition.

 2. Copy the nine pak00?.pk4 files of Doom 3 1.3.1 into Doom3/base/ (on
    the PC they are in the game's folder, under "base"). For the demo,
    copy demo00.pk4 into Doom3/demo/ instead. Leave amiga.pk4, already
    in both drawers, where it is: it is the port's own.

 3. Double-click Doom3. The first start takes a while to load; then the
    main menu opens at 1280x720. A new game's first level takes about a
    minute and a half to load.

 From a Shell, for example:
       Work:Doom3/Doom3 QUALITY=medium W=1920 H=1080

 Options (as Shell arguments or the icon's Tool Types): W= and H= (the
 screen size), QUALITY=low|medium|high|ultra, LIGHTS= (the most lights
 drawn), AUDIO=paula|ahi, SOUND=0, MAP= (start in a level), RESET (the
 first start's settings again). Everything is in the ReadMe in the
 drawer.

 Updating from an earlier version: unpack over the old drawer; your
 data in base/ and demo/ and your settings and saved games in save/
 stay.

 To remove it: delete the Doom3 drawer and Doom3.info. Nothing was
 copied anywhere else.


ARCHIVE CONTENTS
----------------

  Doom3/Doom3            Starts the game (with its icon): runs Doom3.script
  Doom3/Doom3.script     Passes the options on, then starts the game
  Doom3/dhewm3           The game itself
  Doom3/*.cfg            The port's part of the four quality settings
  Doom3/libs/            ogles2.library 1.4 beta 3 (Pi 4 and Pi 3) and
                         its shader compiler v3dglslc
  Doom3/shaders/         The game's shaders, compiled for the Pi 4
  Doom3/base/            For Doom 3's data; holds amiga.pk4, the port's
                         own small data file, and a ReadMe.txt
  Doom3/demo/            For the free demo's data instead; amiga.pk4
  Doom3/licences/        The licences of the parts
  Doom3/source/          The game's complete source code (GPL), packed
                         as Doom3-Amiga-src.lha; not needed to play
  Doom3/ReadMe           The full guide (options, quality, sound,
                         playing, troubleshooting)
  Doom3.info, *.info     Icons


KNOWN LIMITATIONS
-----------------

  * Single player only: no multiplayer.
  * The expansion "Resurrection of Evil" is not supported.
  * Levels take a minute or two to load.
  * Lights beyond the LIGHTS limit (12 by default) are left out, so
    lights can pop in and out as you move; LIGHTS=0 draws them all.
  * A timedemo with the sound off (SOUND=0) crashes: run timedemos with
    sound.


TECHNICAL NOTES
---------------

The game is dhewm3 (d3es-multithread) compiled for the 68k with GCC 16.2
and libnix, with the port's changes as patches: an AmigaOS 3 system
layer, SDL2 (SDL2_68k) for the screen and input, its own small OpenAL
for sound, the renderer on OpenGL ES 2.0, and many changes for speed on
Emu68. ogles2.library (OpenGL ES 2.0, with PiStorm3D's V3D code) drives
the Raspberry Pi's GPU directly; the game's shaders are compiled on the
Amiga by its compiler v3dglslc (Mesa's), or shipped compiled.


HISTORY
-------

  1.0-beta6  ogles2.library 1.4 beta 3 and its shader compiler: the
             one included before refused a Raspberry Pi 3's requests.
             The game's source code in source/; the icons' images for
             AmigaOS 3.0 and 3.1 drawn from the port's own art.
  1.0-beta5  The included ogles2.library is used only on the PiStorm;
             elsewhere (WinUAE) the one installed in LIBS:.
             SetEnv D3_OGLES2 system or drawer chooses.
  1.0-beta4  A full screen on Picasso96 systems that open 32-bit RTG
             modes only as depth 24 (uaegfx in WinUAE), where the game
             opened a window on the Workbench.
  1.0-beta3  The port's own small data file amiga.pk4 in base/ and demo/.
  1.0-beta2  ogles2.library 1.4 beta 2 from the drawer over one in LIBS:;
             demos read into memory before they play; a message instead
             of a crash without game data; shadows off at every quality.
  1.0-beta1  The settings saved and used at every start; 1280x720 by
             default; the game's speed following the frame rate; only
             the screen sizes the system has in the menu; clickable
             in-game screens; colour icons; the Raspberry Pi 3.


